This is already done in Path2D and Path3D for the `curve` property. This applies the same principle to a lot more nodes so that you don't need as many clicks to set up a new node. If you don't need the default-created resource, load another resource in its place (the number of clicks is the same as before).

The resources automatically created are all resources you *must* create at some point for the node/resource to work correctly. Optional resources (e.g. those that act as overrides) are not created automatically to avoid any undesired behavior or performance impact.

This PR also allows `PROPERTY_USAGE_EDITOR_INSTANTIATE_OBJECT` to work with resource hints that list multiple classes `Like,This`. The first class is always selected in this case, as it's assumed to be the most commonly needed one (and is also the first one to display in the dropdown).

This behavior is editor-only, so it doesn't affect nodes/resources created from a script.

For GPUParticles3D, we can benefit further from having custom code to automatically create a billboarded QuadMesh on editor instantiation in a future PR. This code works well when placed at the end of its constructor, but it should be called by something like [`EditorData::instantiate_object_properties()`](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/16d61427cab3a8e43f0a9a8ee724fc176b6433c6/editor/editor_data.cpp#L586-L599) instead so it's not considered as a default value:

```cpp
#ifdef TOOLS_ENABLED
#include "scene/resources/material.h"
#include "scene/resources/primitive_meshes.h"
#endif

// ...

#ifdef TOOLS_ENABLED
	if (Engine::get_singleton()->is_editor_hint() && get_draw_pass_mesh(0) == nullptr) {
		Ref<QuadMesh> quad_mesh = memnew(QuadMesh);
		quad_mesh->set_size(Vector2(0.05, 0.05));

		// Create material to display billboarded texture that follows the color and scale ramps
		// defined in ParticleProcessMaterial.
		Ref<StandardMaterial3D> material = memnew(StandardMaterial3D);
		material->set_shading_mode(BaseMaterial3D::SHADING_MODE_UNSHADED);
		material->set_transparency(BaseMaterial3D::TRANSPARENCY_ALPHA);
		material->set_flag(BaseMaterial3D::FLAG_ALBEDO_FROM_VERTEX_COLOR, true);
		material->set_flag(BaseMaterial3D::FLAG_BILLBOARD_KEEP_SCALE, true);
		material->set_billboard_mode(BaseMaterial3D::BILLBOARD_PARTICLES);

		// Assign a smooth circle texture.
		Ref<GradientTexture2D> texture = memnew(GradientTexture2D);
		texture->set_fill(GradientTexture2D::FILL_RADIAL);
		texture->set_fill_from(Vector2(0.5, 0.5));
		texture->set_fill_to(Vector2(0.5, 0.01));
		Ref<Gradient> gradient = memnew(Gradient);
		gradient->set_color(0, Color(1, 1, 1));
		gradient->set_color(1, Color(1, 1, 1, 0));
		texture->set_gradient(gradient);
		material->set_texture(BaseMaterial3D::TEXTURE_ALBEDO, texture);
		quad_mesh->set_material(material);

		set_draw_pass_mesh(0, quad_mesh);
	}
#endif
```

**Edit:** The above part can be implemented using https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/122058.

